# Φ F x, THE PARTS PUT INTO THE COMPOSED LAYER'S UNIT. One row per resolved part that states
# a demand, carrying that demand already multiplied by the factor.
#
# ⭐⭐ THE CONVERSION IS DIAGONAL, WHICH IS WHY IT MULTIPLIES INSTEAD OF MIXING. Each part
#    converts by its own single factor; nothing here combines two parts' units. That is what
#    lets the sum downstream be a sum at all.
#
# ⛔⛔ AN ABSENT FACTOR MEANS ONE, EXACTLY, AND NEVER "UNKNOWN". A part with no factor is a
#    part whose units already agree. A part whose factor is a TYPED ABSENCE inside the claim
#    is a different document -- somebody who says nobody measured the conversion -- and
#    `coalesce` reads only the first. Confusing them would silently assert a conversion no
#    filer made.
#
# ⛔ AND THE FACTOR IS CORRELATED WITH ITSELF. One factor multiplies both a part's nameplate
#   and its demand, so the two converted intervals move together. Difference them as though
#   they were independent and the spread gets counted twice -- which is why a composed
#   remainder is CONVERTED and never re-derived. See queries/matrices/4-converted-remainder.sql.
# ⛔ IT IS Φ APPLIED TO DEMAND, AND THE `quantity` COLUMN SAYS SO. The relation composes
#   layers/demand.sqlc and nothing else, so `d_low/d_mode/d_high` are converted DEMANDS. A
#   nameplate composition would be the same Φ over layers/nameplate.sqlc and is a sibling file,
#   not a parameter of this one.
-- asrt:Part/asrt:factor applied to the part layer's pm:Demand.
SELECT p.composition, p.composed_layer, p.part_filing, p.part_layer,
       'demand' AS quantity,
       d.d_low  * coalesce(p.factor_low, 1)  AS d_low,
       d.d_mode * coalesce(p.factor_mode, 1) AS d_mode,
       d.d_high * coalesce(p.factor_high, 1) AS d_high
FROM      (
    :compose(composition/parts.sqlc)
) p
JOIN      (
    :compose(layers/demand.sqlc)
) d
       ON d.filing = p.part_filing AND d.layer = p.part_layer
